      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly. Your strongest counter-argument to the position that expansion of executive privilege leads to a lack of accountability is grounded in the U.S. constitutional framework that provides for a system of checks and balances. Specifically, you should stress that while executive privilege is indeed an expression of the separation of powers, the judiciary has established through various rulings that there are limits to this privilege. Counterpoints to the \"erosion of checks and balances\" argument would include precedents such as United States v. Nixon (1974), where the Supreme Court demonstrated its willingness to enforce checks on executive power, ruling that executive privilege could not be used to obstruct justice. Furthermore, you can argue that the War Powers Resolution and Congressional oversight authorities are built-in mechanisms designed to ensure that the executive does not become an unchecked monarchy. These instances show that the legal system has real teeth in protecting against an overreach of the executive, ensuring that there are indeed compelling legal counterweights to unchecked expansion of executive privilege. In your moot court, you can assert that the checks and balances within the system are robust enough to counter the risks of unmitigated executive power concentration.",
